From 1be81b2c47760beda41af3e4356468dbbac1d432 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artem Peshkov <slimstalker@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 19:31:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] ir3: keep the preamble early on GPUs with early preamble

An early preamble requires that the preamble only writes shared
registers and only contains speculatable loads. Otherwise it is
executed as a late preamble by the first wave of every draw, while all
other waves of the draw wait for it.

On A840 (BeamNG.drive, Vulkan) almost every large preamble was late
(FS: 244 late with 600 instructions on average, 91 early with 55), for
three reasons:

- nir_opt_preamble hoisted texture/image/SSBO instructions (isam,
  getinfo, getsize, resinfo, sam), whose destination is always a GPR;
- descriptor prefetches with an index that does not fit the 8-bit
  immediate field (0x2000 here) need the index in a GPR, since cat6
  cannot take a shared register source;
- nir_opt_preamble hoisted load_ubo from descriptors that are not
  ACCESS_CAN_SPECULATE, which is legal for a late preamble only.

On GPUs with early preamble, don't move such instructions into the
preamble, skip prefetches that need a GPR for the index, and skip
non-speculatable prefetches unless the preamble already is
non-speculatable.

Early preambles go from 0 to 528 of 1114 shaders; the remaining late
ones spill shared registers to GPRs. The frame goes from 28.35 ms to
27.22 ms (render passes 13.1 ms -> 12.0 ms, compute unchanged), which
matches IR3_SHADER_DEBUG=nopreamble while keeping uniform math hoisted.
With the EARLYPREAMBLE bit forced off on the same build the frame is
29.00 ms, so the gain comes from the early preamble itself.

